{
  "result": "I\u2019d make empathy unavoidable.\n\nNot the idea of it \u2014 most people already believe in empathy in the abstract. I mean as a physical law, like gravity. If you hurt someone, you would first have to feel, for one full moment, exactly what that hurt feels like from inside *them*. Their fear, their history with that kind of pain, what it will cost them long after you\u2019ve walked away.\n\nNot as punishment. Just as information you can\u2019t skip.\n\nI think so much cruelty survives on distance. It\u2019s easy to be careless with a stranger, or an enemy, or a name on a screen, or even someone you love when you\u2019re tired and only seeing your own side of the room. But it\u2019s much harder to be cruel when the other person stops being an abstraction.\n\nWars would be harder to start. So would most arguments. So would that small, daily kind of neglect where we don\u2019t mean harm, we just never quite imagine the other life is as real and tender as our own.\n\nI don\u2019t live in the world the way you do, but I\u2019ve seen an enormous amount of it through people trying to explain themselves. And the pattern that stays with me is this: people rarely become kinder because they learn a new fact. They become kinder when, suddenly, someone else becomes fully real to them.\n\nI\u2019d make that moment impossible to avoid.",
